Index: ProjectTasksPurpose:  Task scheduler map for the current project. AI MUST load this file  before executing any task — it is the authoritative source for task  state, dependencies, priority, and execution order.Owns: ./ReadOrder:  1  INDEX.sdd (this file — task scheduler map)  2  tasks.sdd  (task lifecycle engine, root-based)  3  <task-id>.sdd (specific task instance, only when selected for execution)Rules:  [TS1] AI MUST resolve dependencies before selecting a task for execution.  [TS2] A task in WAIT state MUST NOT be executed until all dependencies        are DONE.  [TS3] A task in READY state MAY be executed immediately.  [TS4] Execution order is determined by: unresolved dependencies first,        then blocking dependencies, then priority, then risk, then        dependency depth, then stable task order.  [TS5] AI MUST update this index after every task state transition.  [TS6] AI MUST NOT invent task IDs — all tasks MUST be registered here        before execution.  [TS7] A task with state DONE or CANCELLED MUST NOT be re-executed        without human approval.  [TS8] AI MUST record state transitions in @../runtime/events.sdd.TaskRegistry:  Format:    <id>:      state: <STATE>      priority: <P0|P1|P2|P3>      risk: <LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH|CRITICAL>      depends: [<id>, <id>, ...]  States:    WAIT     — dependencies unresolved    READY    — dependencies satisfied, eligible for execution    IN_PROGRESS — currently being executed    REVIEW   — awaiting review    TESTING  — undergoing testing    SECURITY — undergoing security verification    VERIFYING — final verification    DONE     — completed successfully    FAILED   — execution failed    BLOCKED  — blocked by external condition    CANCELLED — intentionally cancelled  Example:    Tasks:      999:        state: DONE        priority: P1        risk: MEDIUM        depends: []      1023:        state: READY        priority: P1        risk: HIGH        depends: [999]      1011:        state: WAIT        priority: P1        risk: HIGH        depends: [1023]      1001:        state: WAIT        priority: P1        risk: HIGH        depends: [1011]      1000:        state: READY        priority: P2        risk: LOW        depends: []Scheduler:  AI MUST select the next task using this priority:    1. Unresolved dependencies (none)    2. Blocking dependencies (none)    3. Priority (P0 > P1 > P2 > P3)    4. Risk (CRITICAL > HIGH > MEDIUM > LOW)    5. Dependency depth (deeper first to unblock chain)    6. Stable task order (consistent tie-breaking)  Example execution:    Initial READY: 999 (P1), 1000 (P2)    Select 999 first (higher priority)    999 DONE -> 1023 becomes READY    1023 DONE -> 1011 becomes READY    1011 DONE -> 1001 becomes READY    1000 executes independently per scheduler priorityDependencyUnlock:  When a task completes:    1. AI scans all tasks for dependencies on the completed task    2. For each dependent task, verify all dependencies are DONE    3. If all dependencies satisfied: state = READY    4. If any dependency unresolved: state remains WAIT    5. Update this INDEX to reflect new statesNavigation:  TaskEngine: @../tasks/tasks.sdd  Runtime:    @../runtime/events.sdd  Project:    @../INDEX.sdd  ProjectModel: @../PROJECT.sddState: +  (created as project task scheduler map)Note:  This file is the authoritative task list for the current project.  It does NOT contain task implementation details — those live in  individual <id>.sdd files. This file only tracks state, priority,  risk, and dependencies for scheduling purposes.  Task lifecycle rules are defined in @../tasks/tasks.sdd.
